00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:14.950
A software developer named
Ray Tomlinson created a way

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:17.740
to send a message to
other connected computers.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:19.863
He called it SNDMSG or send message if

00:06:19.863 --> 00:06:22.090
you put the missing letters back in.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:24.790
The way he did this was
by using the at sign

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:27.460
to separate the user from the computer.

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:31.600
Tomlinson is often noted as
the grandfather of the email

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:34.240
because of this cross
computer messaging system

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:38.230
and very specifically for
making use of that at sign,

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:40.960
what was that symbol even used for before?

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:42.010
Who knows?

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:44.020
Here's where the drama comes in.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:46.450
Now, oftentimes Tomlinson is credited

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:48.340
