00:08:37.176 --> 00:08:39.400
An email address contains two parts.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:43.360
On the left side of that infamous
at symbol is the username

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.940
and on the right side is the domain name.

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:49.270
Now, the way the email finds
its way to you is by making use

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:51.460
of that domain name portion.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.770
The way it's able to do that is with DNS

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:56.110
or domain name system.

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:59.200
Servers that are on the
internet have an IP address.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.120
IP stands for internet protocol.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:03.100
Now, we'll dive into
protocols here in a bit.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:06.850
An IP address traditionally
is four numbers between one

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:10.330
and 255 separated by periods.

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:13.000
The web browser uses DNS
to find the web server

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.430
that hosts the webpage that
you're attempting to view.

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:17.470
That URL contains the domain name

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:19.270
of the place that you're looking at.

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:22.713
A common analogy that's
used to explain this

00:09:22.713 --> 00:09:25.480
is a little dated for
years we've been teaching.

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:27.340
It's like, you know,
it's like the phone book.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:28.780
You know you look up the name

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:30.250
and then you get the phone number,

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:32.009
but when was the last time you ever

00:09:32.009 --> 00:09:34.930
even used the phone book?

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:36.730
Let's try this one on for size.

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:39.760
You can search for a restaurant
in your maps application

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:41.050
and it returns the latitude

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:44.050
and longitude for you as well
as turn by turn directions.

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:46.570
It translates the name of
the place that you're looking

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:49.330
for into map coordinates.

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:51.580
DNS does a similar sort of thing.

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:54.340
It uses the domain name
to look up the IP address

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:56.500
of the server you're requesting.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:58.300
The way this works is that the owners

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:01.270
of a domain must create DNS records

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:03.010
that associate their domain name

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:06.700
with the server's IP addresses,
and I say servers here

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:08.650
because there can be multiple

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:11.080
and we've been talking about web servers,

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:15.280
but there's also email
servers, so what happens is

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:19.390
that the domain owners add
what is known as an MX record,

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:21.880
a mail exchange record or records.

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:24.220
Actually, you can have multiple MX records

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:26.950
and you can prioritize them
so that they get checked

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:28.810
for the user in order.

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:30.340
Now, a thing to remember is

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:33.880
that these early days,
websites were not full-blown

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:35.920
applications like we have these days.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:39.070
These sites were just kind of
like informational billboards.

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:42.250
They were definitely a far
way away from the concept

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:45.070
of a web-based email program like Gmail.

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:48.220
Now, what these early
email adopters were using

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:50.110
were called email clients.

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:52.600
They were actual applications
that ran on your computer

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:54.280
and they're still around today.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:57.730
What you, the user of the
email client would do was

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:00.393
to configure your domain and
then you could use it to send

00:11:00.393 --> 00:11:02.680
and receive messages.

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:06.040
In early cases, messages were
downloaded to your computer

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:08.680
and stored there locally, which of course,

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:11.110
caused problems later when
you got a new computer,

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:12.610
but we weren't there yet.

00:11:12.610 --> 00:11:13.750
We were here.

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:15.130
You've got mail.

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:18.220
AOL would send an actual
CD ROM in the mail

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:21.040
and you'd install it and
it included a mail client

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:24.460
and we were so, so
excited about being able

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:26.140
to communicate with each other.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:27.970
These clients would use the domain name

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:30.790
to find the mail exchanger, but then what?

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:33.580
Great question, and I'm
glad you asked, it's time

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:35.920
that we dive a little bit into the winners

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:37.870
of the Protocol Wars.

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:41.410
A protocol is a set of agreed upon rules

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:43.330
and they aren't just
limited to technology.

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:45.040
We have them in real life too.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:47.200
For instance, if we are meeting each other

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:50.950
for the first time and I stick
out my hand, the protocol

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:52.930
that us humans have established is

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:55.780
that you would put your hand in mine

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:57.220
and we would make a tight squeeze

00:11:57.220 --> 00:11:59.140
and we would move it up and down, right?

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:00.160
A handshake.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:01.810
It's kind of weird when
you think about it,

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:03.520
but a protocol nonetheless.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:07.120
Now, it'd be really strange
if I put my hand out for you

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:09.880
to shake it and then you
did the chicken dance.

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:12.340
I mean, I'd probably
appreciate you doing that,

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:14.770
but you'll for sure be
breaking, breaking the protocol

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:16.780
that we have unspoken set up.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:18.370
Here, let's do another one.

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:19.270
How about the way

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:22.300
that we send actual mail
like through the post office?

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:26.050
Now, the protocol as I
understand it is this.

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:28.870
I put the contents in an envelope

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:32.830
and on that envelope I
write your name and address

00:12:32.830 --> 00:12:36.640
and I also put my return
contact info in the corner.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:39.970
I put a stamp, I drop this in the mailbox

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:42.430
and some post office hero
comes and picks it up.

00:12:42.430 --> 00:12:43.990
It finds its way to you.

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:46.990
You get it and you're like,
oh, hey, this is for me

00:12:46.990 --> 00:12:48.880
and oh look, it's from Craig.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.470
I wonder what it is.

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:53.350
We just successfully ran a real life

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:55.000
mail protocol, snail mail,

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.220
though it's not what
we're here to talk about.

00:12:57.220 --> 00:12:58.720
What we're here to talk about is the

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:01.000
electronic version of that.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.970
How did we possibly
enable everyone to be able

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:05.830
to start sending emails?

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:06.793
Any guesses?

00:13:08.170 --> 00:13:11.530
If you guessed a well-defined
protocol, you got it.

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:12.730
As the internet that we know

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:15.760
and love was being conceived,
it became clear that in order

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:18.073
to make it open and
extensible, it was going

00:13:18.073 --> 00:13:20.170
to need some standards

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:22.510
and the way that these
standards came into being was

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:25.960
by some pretty clever group brainstorming,

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:28.720
a memorandum called an RFC

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:31.990
or a request for comment was created

00:13:31.990 --> 00:13:33.460
and that had a proposal

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:34.870
and it was discussed by a group

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:37.480
of people interested in building a stable

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:38.800
and secure internet.

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:41.320
This group that I'm talking
about specifically here

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:45.340
is the IETF, the Internet
Engineering Task Force,

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:49.030
which I think sounds like
a group of superheroes

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:51.730
and I guess in reality, they truly are.

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:53.650
I know I owe my career

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:55.000
and most of my knowledge

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:58.090
to their early enablement of these ideas.

00:13:58.090 --> 00:14:00.250
There are multiple of
these superhero groups,

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:03.760
the internet cinematic
universe, if you will,

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:05.590
that weigh in on these RFCs and

00:14:05.590 --> 00:14:07.600
after they're approved, they become known

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:09.700
as internet standards.

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:12.100
Now, this RFC process brought us some

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:14.650
of the greatest hits, we're
hanging out in one right now.

00:14:14.650 --> 00:14:16.960
Check out your browser's address bar.

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:19.270
The address starts with HTTP.

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:21.400
That's hypertext transfer protocol

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:24.220
and it was introduced as an RFC

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:27.280
and it became an internet standard,

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:28.420
and these are pretty detailed

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:30.310
and I find them quite fascinating.

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:32.590
I love learning this history,

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:34.450
but I know that's not why you're here.

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:36.700
Check the notes if I just nerd sniped you,

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:39.250
so the internet standard that I'd love

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:42.940
to dive into here is an
early hit, number 10, the one

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:44.980
that enabled us to send email.

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:48.790
SMTP or Simple Mail Transfer Protocol.

00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:51.820
You might not agree that
it is exactly simple,

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:55.120
but it is the defacto way to
get your mail transferred.

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:58.870
Now, conceptually, you have
a sender and a receiver.

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:00.130
They introduce themselves

00:15:00.130 --> 00:15:04.000
and do a handshake, oh hey, just like us.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.490
It's a plain text protocol,
which means you can kind

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:08.740
of read it with a little more information

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:11.170
that there's a set of SMTP commands

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:14.530
and status codes that could
come back in response.

00:15:14.530 --> 00:15:16.360
They help you build the envelope

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:18.340
and explain what it is that you're trying

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:20.440
to send and to whom.

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:23.320
If you're feeling brave, you
can actually make use of this

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:25.690
through a tool like Telnet,
which will allow you

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:28.330
to communicate with a remote server.

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:32.410
SMTP is the internet standard
used on the sending side,

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:35.890
but there are also protocols
that allow mail clients

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:39.940
to retrieve the email from
their mail server, POP,

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:43.480
or post office protocol
often now called POP 3,

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:46.840
which is the current version,
helps you as a reader

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:48.850
of the email receive a copy

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:51.520
of it from your server side mailbox.

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:53.920
These days, it's hard to imagine,

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:56.050
but there was actually a
time when we weren't always

00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:57.580
able to be online.

00:15:57.580 --> 00:15:59.500
Now, this protocol enables you

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:01.480
to download messages from your mail

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:04.420
server to your local mailbox.

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:06.100
You wanted this so that you could deal

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:07.630
with them when you were offline.

00:16:07.630 --> 00:16:09.130
Now, original attempts here

00:16:09.130 --> 00:16:11.890
actually remove the message
from the mail server

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:12.970
after it was downloaded

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:15.400
to the user's client site application.

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:17.440
Nowadays, this can cause some issues

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:20.380
because we access our
email from multiple devices

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:24.550
and things can get outta
sync, which leads us to IMAP

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:27.610
or the internet message access protocol.

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:29.470
This allows for better synchronization

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:31.060
and internal messaging,

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:32.710
and while you probably aren't here

00:16:32.710 --> 00:16:35.050
to learn about the
receiving side of things,

00:16:35.050 --> 00:16:39.370
you will see both POP and IMAP
quite a bit in this space.

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:41.650
Now, I don't want you to
feel like you need to be able

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:43.960
to explain how these work
without looking 'em up,

00:16:43.960 --> 00:16:47.110
but I do want you to recognize
them when you see them.

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:48.850
You'll encounter these in documentation

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:50.770
and now you should have a basic

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:52.240
understanding of what they are.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:55.300
Remember, if you ever want
a refresher, check the notes

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:56.890
for more info.

00:16:56.890 --> 00:17:00.130
So now we have all these
wonderful programs.

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:04.090
Anyone who implements the SMTP
protocol can send a message.

00:17:04.090 --> 00:17:07.210
You can actually even do this
dance from the command line on

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:09.943
your computer or directly from code,

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:15.130
and then of course the reason
we can't have nice things,

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:16.600
the bad actors showed up.

00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:17.950
You guessed it.

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:21.583
It's time to talk about
spamming and spoofing.

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:24.670
Earlier when I asked you

00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:27.070
to drop your first email
address into the comments,

00:17:27.070 --> 00:17:29.230
I bet you had a weird
feeling about doing that.

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:31.600
Something along the lines
of, if I put this in here,

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:32.740
I'm pretty sure I'm gonna get

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:34.660
so many emails that I don't want.

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:37.630
Now, as you know, we call
those emails the ones

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:39.520
that you don't want, spam.

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:40.810
Why would we do this?

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:44.713
Who wouldn't want this
delicious stuff, right?

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:50.110
Even though this feels like some sort

00:17:50.110 --> 00:17:53.770
of advanced prop dad joke, the
term that we use for emails

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:58.570
that we don't want is actually
rooted in this meat in a can.

00:17:58.570 --> 00:18:00.220
Well, sort of.

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:02.290
The British comedy troupe Monty Python

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:04.480
had a bunch of famous sketches

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:08.050
and one of their most absurd
involves a person attempting

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:10.180
to order a meal and they noticed

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:11.887
that everything on the
menu had spam in it,

00:18:11.887 --> 00:18:13.393
and that person asks.

00:18:14.440 --> 00:18:16.330
- Have got anything without spam in it?

00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:18.040
Well, a spam egg, sausage

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:19.733
and spam, it's not got much spam in it.

00:18:19.733 --> 00:18:21.850
- And asks.
- Why can't you have egg,

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:23.470
bacon and spam and sausage?

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:24.400
- That's got spam in it.

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:27.468
- It's not as much as
spam egg sausage and spam.

00:18:27.468 --> 00:18:30.741
- Look, could I have egg, bacon, spam

00:18:30.741 --> 00:18:33.070
and sausage without the spam?

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:34.540
- Because everything has spam,

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:36.250
spam and more spam.

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:39.793
Eventually, Vikings show
up and chaos ensues.

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:46.090
And chaos did ensue on
the email front too.

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:47.290
Spam was everywhere.

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:49.840
Those well-defined
protocols worked really,

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:51.880
really well at connecting people

00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:55.060
and bad actors as we
call them now, saw this

00:18:55.060 --> 00:18:58.390
as an opportunity to communicate
what they were selling

00:18:58.390 --> 00:19:01.960
and our inboxes were just
bombarded with offers

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:03.760
for quite literally everything.

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:05.680
There were email lists that were for sale

00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:08.470
and oh boy, if you got
on one of those lists,

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:11.050
you got so much spam.

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:13.510
Those lists are still for sale
by the way, don't buy them.

00:19:13.510 --> 00:19:14.740
It might sound like a good idea,

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:17.530
but it is a very, very bad idea,

00:19:17.530 --> 00:19:20.410
but in the beginning,
it's important to remember

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:22.090
that there were no real standards

00:19:22.090 --> 00:19:23.560
or even etiquette at the time,

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:25.750
so everyone was probably a little guilty

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:29.500
of over sending messages that
receivers didn't really want.

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:31.450
There were all sorts of lawsuits,

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:32.980
people actually got arrested.

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:33.880
Check the notes for more.

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:35.560
There's so much drama, right?

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:39.850
Finally in 2003, the FTC got involved

00:19:39.850 --> 00:19:43.750
and get this, the act is
called Controlling the Assault

00:19:43.750 --> 00:19:46.510
of Non-Solicited Pornography and Marketing

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:48.970
or CAN SPAM for short.

00:19:48.970 --> 00:19:51.070
I see what you did there FTC.

00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:52.300
Now this act applies

00:19:52.300 --> 00:19:55.360
to all commercial email
messages, not just bulk ones.

00:19:55.360 --> 00:19:58.090
The non-compliant penalties
can be pretty hefty,

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:01.180
up to $50,000.

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:03.310
These introduced some rules
that are pretty straightforward

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:05.140
and really made a difference in the amount

00:20:05.140 --> 00:20:06.550
of spam that we were fighting.

00:20:06.550 --> 00:20:08.530
Let's talk through them really quick.

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:11.470
Don't use false or misleading
header information.

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:14.320
Now all of the information on
your email envelope must be

00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:18.190
accurate and identify the person
or business as the sender.

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:19.990
Don't use deceptive subject lines.

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:21.970
Be careful with the click bait.

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:24.850
You have to make sure that
your messages is about

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:27.010
what the subject claims.

00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:29.080
Identify the message as an ad.

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:31.570
You need to let people know
that you're sending an ad.

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:34.210
There are best practices here
and there's more in the notes.

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:36.250
Tell recipients where you're located.

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:39.100
You have to provide a
valid street address.

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:40.120
Tell recipients how

00:20:40.120 --> 00:20:43.450
to opt out a receiving future
marketing email from you.

00:20:43.450 --> 00:20:45.010
You have to let your users tell you

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:46.720
that they don't want any more messages

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:48.250
like the one that you just sent.

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:50.980
You need to do this, again,
best practices are in the notes.

00:20:50.980 --> 00:20:53.620
Remember that subscribers
and members can opt out

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:55.240
of marketing emails too.

00:20:55.240 --> 00:20:57.850
If you have an app that
makes use of subscriptions

00:20:57.850 --> 00:21:01.060
and memberships, you
have to also make sure

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:01.893
that they can opt out.

00:21:01.893 --> 00:21:04.150
Now, this one can be a little tricky,

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:07.270
but don't forget to let
them not receive the emails.

00:21:07.270 --> 00:21:09.700
And honor opt-out request promptly.

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:11.200
This can get a little tricky too.

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:12.880
If you offer opt-out,

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:14.950
it has to work for at least 30 days

00:21:14.950 --> 00:21:19.270
and you have to honor it
within a minimum of 10 days.

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:21.640
Monitor what others are
doing on your behalf.

00:21:21.640 --> 00:21:24.520
The law makes clear that even
if you hire another company

00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:26.170
to handle your email marketing,

00:21:26.170 --> 00:21:28.990
you can't contract away
your legal responsibility

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:30.430
to comply with the law.

00:21:30.430 --> 00:21:34.510
The CAN SPAM Act made a huge
dent in the emails being sent,

00:21:34.510 --> 00:21:38.170
but spam still maintained
a very impressive sometimes

00:21:38.170 --> 00:21:41.380
upwards of 70% of all email traffic.

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:43.390
Now, luckily for those of
us with email addresses,

00:21:43.390 --> 00:21:45.880
the spam percentage is
constantly dropping.

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:48.220
Our email providers are making it harder

00:21:48.220 --> 00:21:51.250
and harder for the bad actors
to get those bad emails

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:53.320
to land in your inbox.

00:21:53.320 --> 00:21:54.700
Can you do me a favor?

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:57.130
I want you to go and
look at your spam folder.

00:21:57.130 --> 00:21:58.360
Just take a quick look in there.

00:21:58.360 --> 00:21:59.710
Go ahead and pause me and then

00:21:59.710 --> 00:22:01.560
take a peek and then come back to me.

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:06.940
Pretty gross right?

00:22:06.940 --> 00:22:08.980
Now however, did you see in there

00:22:08.980 --> 00:22:10.630
there's probably some messages

00:22:10.630 --> 00:22:13.210
that were incorrectly marked spam.

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:14.980
Providers have done
the best that they can,

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:19.090
but sometimes wanted messages
actually end up in there.

00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:21.400
As someone who is considering
getting email running

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:25.120
for your application, you don't
want this to happen to you.

00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:27.460
Well talk through some best
practices through the rest

00:22:27.460 --> 00:22:30.550
of this course of how to get
your message into the inbox

00:22:30.550 --> 00:22:32.800
and out of the spam folder.

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:37.090
This is called deliverability
and it is truly an art form.

00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:39.370
Now, one of the first
points in the CAN SPAM Act

00:22:39.370 --> 00:22:41.530
talks about not faking the sender.

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:44.890
Unfortunately, SMTP doesn't
provide authentication,

00:22:44.890 --> 00:22:48.610
so it's relatively easy to
pretend to be someone else.

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:51.280
If we flip back to our snail mail example,

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:54.280
I can swap out my return
address to whatever I want

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:56.830
and maybe that'd make you wanna open it.

00:22:56.830 --> 00:23:00.400
Well, I could do the same
thing with my email envelope.

00:23:00.400 --> 00:23:03.310
Pretending to be someone you
are not is called spoofing

00:23:03.310 --> 00:23:04.900
and it's a bummer.

00:23:04.900 --> 00:23:07.420
The good news is there
are ways of handling this

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:10.060
and employing suggested
methods will greatly help you

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:12.250
with your deliverability journey.

00:23:12.250 --> 00:23:13.810
Let's take a quick break

00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:16.150
and when we come back,
let's take a look at some

00:23:16.150 --> 00:23:18.403
of these authentication mechanisms.

00:23:24.781 --> 00:23:26.283
Mm.

00:23:26.283 --> 00:23:27.804
Mm.

00:23:27.804 --> 00:23:28.794
Mm.

00:23:28.794 --> 00:23:33.794
(peaceful music)
(water whooshing)

00:23:53.410 --> 00:23:56.080
As you can imagine, it's
important to the receivers

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:58.150
of your emails that they can identify

00:23:58.150 --> 00:24:00.220
that the emails actually from you.

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:02.920
As more and more spoofing
happened, technology was added

00:24:02.920 --> 00:24:05.800
to give you a little more power
in making this dream happen.

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:07.990
For many years, these
techniques were known

00:24:07.990 --> 00:24:09.430
as a best practice.

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:11.470
These days, however, they're basically

00:24:11.470 --> 00:24:13.330
slowly becoming required.

00:24:13.330 --> 00:24:15.160
Changes are happening in 2024

00:24:15.160 --> 00:24:17.500
that will make all senders start sending

00:24:17.500 --> 00:24:21.160
properly authenticated emails
to be considered trustworthy.

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:24.400
The way this works is with surprise,

00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:25.630
a group of more acronyms.

00:24:25.630 --> 00:24:26.463
I apologize.

00:24:26.463 --> 00:24:28.600
I know that there's a lot in this space.

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:30.640
Now, I want you to approach
these the same way.

00:24:30.640 --> 00:24:32.230
I don't want you to feel like you have

00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:34.240
to understand them completely,

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:36.220
but it will certainly benefit you

00:24:36.220 --> 00:24:37.900
to be able to recognize them.

00:24:37.900 --> 00:24:41.020
You'll find these acronyms
and blogs and dropdown menus

00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:44.200
and function parameters
often without any explanation

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:46.879
whatsoever, and yes, I realize

00:24:46.879 --> 00:24:50.260
that the more pedantically
focused learners may say

00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:52.840
that these are actually
initialisms and non acronyms,

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:55.780
but I say any initialism
can be an acronym.

00:24:55.780 --> 00:24:56.980
If you try hard enough.

00:24:56.980 --> 00:25:01.960
SPF or SPF stands for
Sender Policy Framework.

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:04.240
It can be set up by
adding an additional DNS

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:05.890
record for your mail server.

00:25:05.890 --> 00:25:08.050
This specifies who can send messages down

00:25:08.050 --> 00:25:10.150
to a specific IP address.

00:25:10.150 --> 00:25:13.030
This makes it way more
challenging for spoofers to try

00:25:13.030 --> 00:25:15.190
and manipulate your envelope.

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:19.090
DKIM or Domain Keys Identified
Mail is similar to SPF.

00:25:19.090 --> 00:25:21.580
You publish your public
key as a DNS record,

00:25:21.580 --> 00:25:23.950
but DKIM adds an additional layer.

00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:26.830
You sign each and every message.

00:25:26.830 --> 00:25:29.170
The receiver can use the
signature to validate

00:25:29.170 --> 00:25:31.390
that the message has
not been tampered with.

00:25:31.390 --> 00:25:33.370
Most mail clients provide a way for you

00:25:33.370 --> 00:25:35.890
to see the original
message as it was sent.

00:25:35.890 --> 00:25:39.040
For instance, in Gmail, I can
open up the additional menu

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:40.660
and choose show original.

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:43.240
Here you can see if the SPF check passed

00:25:43.240 --> 00:25:45.580
and also what happened with the DKIM

00:25:45.580 --> 00:25:48.280
and if you want to dig,
you can peep at all

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:49.730
the headers that came across.

00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:54.610
DMARC or domain based message
authentication reporting

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:58.000
and conformance came on the scene in 2015

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:01.090
and it moves to further
take on spoofing issues.

00:26:01.090 --> 00:26:02.663
It extends both SPF

00:26:02.663 --> 00:26:05.740
and DKIM to allow the
receiving email server

00:26:05.740 --> 00:26:08.710
to authenticate the incoming message.

00:26:08.710 --> 00:26:11.140
A DNS DMARC record is added

00:26:11.140 --> 00:26:13.480
and in it it defines how you would like

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:16.840
to handle any message that
didn't meet the specification.

00:26:16.840 --> 00:26:20.350
It can also specify how
strict things need to be.

00:26:20.350 --> 00:26:23.260
Email domain owners can
get a daily report of

00:26:23.260 --> 00:26:25.660
what messages failed to pass.

00:26:25.660 --> 00:26:29.980
More in the notes on DMARC,
again coming very soon,

00:26:29.980 --> 00:26:33.130
everyone will need to adhere
to these to be able to send

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:34.840
to Gmail and Yahoo.

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:37.690
Now, the advice is that
every other provider is going

00:26:37.690 --> 00:26:40.510
to follow their lead, so make
sure that you check the notes

00:26:40.510 --> 00:26:43.573
for how to embrace these email
authentication protocols.

