00:36:20.740 --> 00:36:22.840
and they have ways of getting your package

00:36:22.840 --> 00:36:24.070
there no matter what.

00:36:24.070 --> 00:36:27.010
They have worked through the
workflows and are reliable.

00:36:27.010 --> 00:36:29.770
In fact, they'll even send status updates

00:36:29.770 --> 00:36:32.530
as your package is en
route to that destination.

00:36:32.530 --> 00:36:35.590
Oh, if you don't mind me
popping outta that analogy,

00:36:35.590 --> 00:36:38.950
let's talk about how rich
the events that happen are

00:36:38.950 --> 00:36:40.303
when you send an email.

00:36:41.470 --> 00:36:42.880
When an email gets sent,

00:36:42.880 --> 00:36:46.180
there is information that is
most likely made available

00:36:46.180 --> 00:36:49.300
to you via your email API of choice.

00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:52.630
Now, a common approach to
this is to use webhooks.

00:36:52.630 --> 00:36:54.190
Webhooks are like a reverse API.

00:36:54.190 --> 00:36:55.996
Instead of you calling the API.

00:36:55.996 --> 00:36:58.510
The API calls you when the event occurs.

00:36:58.510 --> 00:37:00.700
Now you write code that handles it.

00:37:00.700 --> 00:37:03.940
Email has so many great
events that you can use

00:37:03.940 --> 00:37:07.330
to improve your deliverability
and your engagement.

00:37:07.330 --> 00:37:11.080
You'll see here these delivery
events like process, dropped,

00:37:11.080 --> 00:37:12.550
delivered, deferred and bounced.

00:37:12.550 --> 00:37:15.460
They're super handy in
building reporting tools.

00:37:15.460 --> 00:37:17.950
You also have super
powerful engagement events

00:37:17.950 --> 00:37:19.630
like open and click.

00:37:19.630 --> 00:37:22.840
This information here shows who they are

00:37:22.840 --> 00:37:24.970
and even what they clicked.

00:37:24.970 --> 00:37:26.620
Now, this can be super
helpful if you're trying

00:37:26.620 --> 00:37:28.330
to attract a certain audience.

00:37:28.330 --> 00:37:30.700
There's all sorts of excellent information

00:37:30.700 --> 00:37:34.630
that you can process and if
your service provides it,

00:37:34.630 --> 00:37:37.780
you can actually see when
people report your email as spam

00:37:37.780 --> 00:37:39.910
or if they choose to unsubscribe.

00:37:39.910 --> 00:37:41.830
Now, most likely, your
service provides a way

00:37:41.830 --> 00:37:45.460
to manage this, but these
can be additionally helpful.

00:37:45.460 --> 00:37:48.550
I love seeing what people
build with these events.

00:37:48.550 --> 00:37:50.500
It really helps you to build healthy

00:37:50.500 --> 00:37:52.600
and reputable email campaigns.

00:37:52.600 --> 00:37:54.610
Now, take a moment and think about

00:37:54.610 --> 00:37:57.391
how this might benefit you
in whatever brought you

00:37:57.391 --> 00:37:59.680
to this course in the first place.

00:37:59.680 --> 00:38:00.970
Would it be helpful to track your

00:38:00.970 --> 00:38:03.070
transactional message delivery?

00:38:03.070 --> 00:38:04.420
What about if you're thinking about

00:38:04.420 --> 00:38:05.980
sending marketing emails?

00:38:05.980 --> 00:38:07.690
Wouldn't it be awesome to be able

00:38:07.690 --> 00:38:11.140
to track which message
is landing the best?

00:38:11.140 --> 00:38:12.850
You could test out all sorts of things.

00:38:12.850 --> 00:38:15.700
You could probably use this
data to answer the question,

00:38:15.700 --> 00:38:17.650
am I sending the right message

00:38:17.650 --> 00:38:20.200
to the right person at the right time

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:22.150
with the right frequency?

00:38:22.150 --> 00:38:24.640
Now, while I got your brain activated,

00:38:24.640 --> 00:38:27.403
let's take things one step further.

00:38:28.360 --> 00:38:31.330
So not only can you write
code to handle the events

00:38:31.330 --> 00:38:33.250
that happen when you send a message, some

00:38:33.250 --> 00:38:36.280
of these email APIs
actually let you process

00:38:36.280 --> 00:38:37.840
incoming messages.

00:38:37.840 --> 00:38:40.870
I've seen some super cool
stuff built with this idea.

00:38:40.870 --> 00:38:43.300
One of my favorite implementations
that I can think of

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:45.970
of all time is Expensify.

00:38:45.970 --> 00:38:49.990
I can email a photo of a
receipt into Expensify.

00:38:49.990 --> 00:38:53.710
It receives my message,
processes the photo using OCR

00:38:53.710 --> 00:38:56.260
or Optical Character Resolution,

00:38:56.260 --> 00:38:59.380
and because it knows who I
am from my email address,

00:38:59.380 --> 00:39:03.070
it can create an expense report
in a draft state filled out

00:39:03.070 --> 00:39:04.750
with my expense item.

00:39:04.750 --> 00:39:07.090
They send me back a message
with a link to the report.

00:39:07.090 --> 00:39:09.250
It's beautiful.

00:39:09.250 --> 00:39:12.070
I think with the approachability
of AI these days,

00:39:12.070 --> 00:39:13.420
we are going to see more

00:39:13.420 --> 00:39:16.390
and more of these inbound
email-based applications.

00:39:16.390 --> 00:39:18.610
Now, I know it's outta
the scope of this course,

00:39:18.610 --> 00:39:20.800
but I just wanted to share
my enthusiasm for it.

00:39:20.800 --> 00:39:24.010
Check the notes for more
information on this concept.

00:39:24.010 --> 00:39:25.090
You did it.

00:39:25.090 --> 00:39:26.740
You made it to the end of the course.

00:39:26.740 --> 00:39:29.800
I hope you had a fun time and
learned a lot about the art

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:32.770
and science necessary to
make email work for you.

00:39:32.770 --> 00:39:34.960
This is a journey

00:39:34.960 --> 00:39:37.720
and I can tell you already
you're off to a great start.

00:39:37.720 --> 00:39:39.640
Please keep me posted on your progress.

00:39:39.640 --> 00:39:42.220
I take your feedback very seriously

00:39:42.220 --> 00:39:43.600
and I'd love to hear from you.

00:39:43.600 --> 00:39:46.060
Please check the notes for
how to get in touch with me.

00:39:46.060 --> 00:39:48.933
Thanks so much for hanging out,
and we'll see you real soon.

